# Production overrides, read by scripts/push-github-secrets.sh.
#
# Copy to `.env.production` (gitignored) and fill in only the keys whose value
# differs from your local `.env`. Everything you leave out here comes from
# `.env` as usual — this file is an overlay, not a second copy.
#
# Why it exists: `.env` is a development file. Two of its values are actively
# harmful in production, and both are the kind of mistake that produces a
# confusing symptom rather than an error. push-github-secrets.sh refuses to
# promote either one, and points here.

# Where the deployed application actually lives. Auth.js builds every OAuth
# redirect from this, and its session cookie is Secure — so a localhost or
# plain-http value means nobody can sign in, with no message saying why.
#
# It must also be an authorized redirect URI on the Google OAuth client (see
# docs/runbooks/google-cloud-setup.md); the Vercel domain alone is not enough.
AUTH_URL=https://attendance-beige-kappa.vercel.app

# Leave EMPTY in production. `1` lets API routes return internal error detail
# to the browser. Useful locally, an information leak on a public deployment.
APP_DEBUG_ERRORS=

# Everything else — AUTH_SECRET, the Google client, the Supabase keys,
# GOOGLE_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY — is normally identical to `.env` and does not
# belong here. Override one only if production genuinely uses a different value
# (a separate Supabase project, a separate Google OAuth client).
#
# One that is worth thinking about rather than copying by reflex:
#
#   GOOGLE_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY
#     If production uses a different key from local, that is fine and arguably
#     better. But CLAUDE.md is explicit that rotating it invalidates every
#     stored connection with no re-encryption path — so pick it once, before
#     anyone signs in, and then leave it alone.
